A||N||11||85fierose||piston rings||||1||
Z||000000||85fierose||02-23-2000||02:41 PM||||Is it possible to remove the head on a 2.5 liter 85 fiero and stroke the engine by turning the wheels while in gear enough to raise the pistons out of the cylinders so that i may remove the old rings and put in new ones. I am trying to install new piston rings and avoid pulling the whole engine. please email me if you know. thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||HF6582||02-23-2000||03:05 PM||||I wish it was that easy. No you can't do it that way. You will have to take the rod bolts loose and then you can slide the pistons out.<P>I always feel that if you are replacing one internal part of the motor that the rest can't be that far behind. <P>Scott <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||02-23-2000||04:12 PM||||I have to agree.  You will have to remove the oil pan in order to remove the connecting rod caps and push the pistons up out of the cylinders.  Next you will have to consider how you are going to prepare the cylinders for new rings.  At the very least the cylinders should be honed to provide the proper finish for the new rings to seat properly.  This assumes that the cylinders don't need to be bored oversize due to wear.  My experience has been that if the engine is worn enough to need new rings, then you are FAR better off removing it and giving it a proper rebuild!  I doubt you will be satisfied with the results if you try to take many shortcuts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||02-23-2000||04:30 PM||||You know, a good Iron Duke is pretty cheap, because everybody wants a 2.8/3.1/3.4, 3800, 3.4 TDC, Northstar, etc.<P>It might be easier and cheaper for you to replace your engine than to rebuild it.  I bet any of the reputable Fiero engine swappers have good Dukes just sitting around that they'd let go for a couple hundred bucks.  Or try posting in The Mall - you never know what Fieroholics have sitting out in the barn.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||02-23-2000||04:31 PM||||You know, a good Iron Duke is pretty cheap, because everybody wants a 2.8/3.1/3.4, 3800, 3.4 TDC, Northstar, etc.<P>It might be easier and cheaper for you to replace your engine than to rebuild it.  I bet any of the reputable Fiero engine swappers have good Dukes just sitting around that they'd let go for a couple hundred bucks.  Or try posting in The Mall - you never know what Fieroholics have sitting out in the barn.||||reg||1
Z||000005||85fierose||02-23-2000||05:18 PM||||yea i figured that i would have to pull the whole engine to do the rings. I was just looking for a way to do it quick. the car has  3x to much hydrocarbons in the exhaust and after replacing alot of the sensors i just figured it was worn rings burning oil. Could there possibly be another explanation? Maybe a valve sticking that i could check without pulling the whole engine. Thanks for your responces.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mwbackus||02-23-2000||05:43 PM||||A compression check or leak down test will be a much better indicator of bad rings than an emissions test.  A three fold increase in HC levels is not uncommon for a high milleage engine with a failed cat. converter.  You may want to try replaceing the cat. first if your problem is simply a failed emmisions test.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||02-23-2000||05:48 PM||||Unless you're leaving a trail of blue smoke wherever you go I think Mike's suggestion of trying the Cat first is a good one.<P>David.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||02-23-2000||09:47 PM||||Let me ask the obvious. Is the engine burning a lot of oil? If not, then it's probably not the rings. If it is burning oil, it may either be worn rings or bad valve guides. Look in the air cleaner housing. If there is a puddle of oil in there, then it's probably the rings. A compression check is another way to tell. Take a set of readings, then squirt a little oil into the spark plug holes. If the numbers increase, it's the rings.||||reg||1
Z||000009||85fierose||02-24-2000||08:42 PM||||Yea i am going to do a compression test this weekend. THe engine isn's burning any oil from what i can tell of the dip stick but the exhaust looks smokey to me even after it warms up.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||02-24-2000||10:55 PM||||If it's a dark sooty exhaust, you might be running a little rich. That'll mean a problem somewhere. Any check engine lights?||||reg||1
Z||000011||85fierose||02-25-2000||12:44 AM||||nope no engine lights and no ecm codes either. I have replaced the tps,map,iac sensors. I was thinking worn rings becuase i don't have any electrical errors codes stored in the ecm. I guess only a compression test will tell. any idea on what a good pressure is? Thanks||||reg||1
